$article=['date'=>'2026-11-17','slug'=>'dyslexia-assessment-multilingual-india','tag'=>'Learning Disabilities','category'=>'Learning Disabilities / Dyslexia','breadcrumb'=>'Dyslexia Assessment in India','meta_title'=>'Dyslexia Assessment in Multilingual and Multi-Script India','description'=>'Learn why dyslexia assessment in India must consider every language and writing system a child uses, educational exposure, teaching and cultural context.','h1'=>'Dyslexia in a Bilingual, Multi-Script India: Why Assessment Is Not One-Size-Fits-All','dek'=>'Difficulty in English, Hindi and another school language cannot be interpreted as though exposure and writing systems were identical.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Dyslexia assessment in India must distinguish a reading disorder from limited exposure, differences between writing systems and unequal instruction. Bilingualism does not cause dyslexia, but testing a multilingual child only through English can produce an incomplete or misleading picture.</p>
<h2>Why script matters</h2><p>English uses an alphabetic system with many inconsistent sound-letter relationships. Devanagari and other Indian scripts organise sounds differently. A child may show different surface errors across scripts while underlying phonological or fluency difficulties appear in both.</p>
<h2>Start with the language history</h2><p>An assessor should ask which languages the child hears, speaks, reads and writes; when each was introduced; the language of instruction; school changes; attendance; and access to teaching. Proficiency is not captured by the label “bilingual.”</p>
<h2>Assess relevant skills across languages</h2><p>Evidence may include reading accuracy and fluency, spelling, comprehension, sound processing, rapid retrieval, writing and response to instruction. Family and teacher information helps show whether difficulty persists across contexts.</p>
<h2>Use culturally and linguistically appropriate tools</h2><p>UNESCO describes DALI, developed by India&rsquo;s National Brain Research Centre, as a set of screening and assessment tools for Indian languages. Tools must be used within their training, age, language and norming limits. Translation of an English test does not automatically make its scores valid.</p>
<h2>What a useful report should explain</h2><p>The report should separate lack of opportunity from a persistent learning difficulty, state limitations, describe strengths and translate findings into teaching and accommodation recommendations for the child&rsquo;s actual curriculum.</p>
<div class="callout-card"><h3>The right question</h3><p>Do not ask whether two languages confused the child. Ask whether reading development is unexpectedly difficult given exposure and instruction in each language.</p></div>
<p>Learn about <a href="/learning-disabilities/learning-disability-assessment">learning disability assessment at eMbrace</a>.</p>
HTML,'faqs'=>[['Does learning two languages cause dyslexia?','No. Bilingualism does not cause dyslexia. Assessment must still consider proficiency, exposure, instruction and reading patterns in every language and script the child uses.'],['Should dyslexia testing be done only in English?','Not when the child learns through multiple languages. English-only results may miss strengths, overidentify limited exposure or fail to detect patterns present across scripts.'],['What is DALI?','DALI is the Dyslexia Assessment for Languages of India, developed through the National Brain Research Centre with screening and assessment tools for several Indian languages.'],['Can dyslexia look different across writing systems?','Yes. Surface errors may differ because scripts represent sounds differently. Assessment examines underlying reading processes alongside language-specific accuracy, fluency, spelling and educational experience.']],
